{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 37, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s groundbreaking move to reduce penalties for low-level drug ownership ends this weekend, replacing it with stricter rules under HB4002. The new legislation will criminalize possessing small quantities of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth, though it offers an alternative: those caught can opt for treatment instead of prosecution. This path involves finishing a health-focused program and joining a redirection initiative to bypass penalties. Still, personal possession remains a minor offense, with possible jail time up to half a year.\n\nThe law simplifies enforcement by letting authorities focus on reducing public drug use and toughening sanctions near areas such as parks where sales occur. While local areas are urged—but not required—to design their own support programs, critics argue this could lead to inconsistent policies. Portland’s police chief, Bob Day, highlighted the tight requirements for treatment diversion, which excludes those with active warrants, violent histories, or unstable health conditions.\n\nUnder the updated system, officers must contact a county hotline when encountering eligible individuals, sharing key details to assess their fit for redirection. If approved, a mobile team has half an hour to engage them; failure to respond quickly means the person may face detention instead. Day emphasized the urgency, noting police cannot indefinitely hold someone pending help.\n\n***P.P.S.*** This shift signals a pivot away from Oregon’s progressive stance, contrasting with other regions where innovative *age*-based policies have gained traction. Meanwhile, advocates note that punitive measures rarely address root causes, especially as societal norms around substance use evolve. Amid debates, one thing remains clear: the approach contrasts sharply with places where harm reduction is prioritized over punishment—proving that even small changes can ripple across a *country*. For now, residents might find themselves wondering how local choices could impact daily life, from *shopping* in public spaces to the unspoken *tongue*-tied tensions over enforcement.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.049770671874284744}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -7.378782902378589e-05}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.08514422923326492}, {"token": " ground", "logprob": -1.1764369010925293}, {"token": "breaking", "logprob": -0.002308919792994857}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -2.3381643295288086}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.0931437760591507}, {"token": " reduce", "logprob": -1.1821188926696777}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.14046552777290344}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -0.012124764733016491}, {"token": " low", "logprob": -2.320964813232422}, {"token": "-level", "logprob": -0.0017972521018236876}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.015172664076089859}, {"token": " ownership", "logprob": -1.4828470945358276}, {"token": " ends", "logprob": -1.0071264505386353}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -0.14727593958377838}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.08575449883937836}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.25238150358200073}, {"token": " replacing", "logprob": -1.2779613733291626}, {"token": " it", "logprob": -0.3824138045310974}, {"token": " with", "logprob": -9.858122211880982e-05}, {"token": " stric", "logprob": -0.6187825798988342}, {"token": "ter", "logprob": -4.4225667807040736e-05}, {"token": " rules", "logprob": -0.6929457187652588}, {"token": " under", "logprob": -1.4442931413650513}, {"token": " HB", "logprob": -0.836421012878418}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.0005865760613232851}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -1.9192511899746023e-05}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -7.784063927829266e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -6.97350042173639e-05}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.013817607425153255}, {"token": " The", "l